 "doc": {
  "type": "doc",
  "content": [
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -12,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 2,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "a.",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "tabPt": 12
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Vilazodone is not a first-line agent per Canadian guidelines due to lack of head-to-head or relapse data and need to titrate and take with food."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "1"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -12,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 2,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "b.",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "tabPt": 12
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "italic"
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Auvelity "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "(US) contains bupropion and dextromethorphan.  Dextromethorphan is an NMDA (N-methyl-D-aspartate) receptor antagonist, but its mechanism in depression is unclear."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "32"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "  Bupropion boosts dextromethorphan levels via CYP2D6 inhibition."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "32"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "  There is no proof that dextromethorphan alone is effective for depression.  There is no proof that "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "italic"
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Auvelity "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "is better than standard-dose bupropion (e.g., bupropion 150 mg twice or 300 mg once daily).  "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "italic"
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Auvelity "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "use with other dextromethorphan-containing products (e.g., cough or cold medicine) could cause neuropsychiatric adverse effects (e.g., psychosis, stupor, seizures)."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "32"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "  Serotonin syndrome could result from extra dextromethorphan use, or "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "italic"
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Auvelity "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "use with serotonergic drugs (e.g., linezolid, serotonergic antidepressants)."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "32,33"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -12,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 2,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "c.",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "tabPt": 12
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10.6
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Wholesale Acquisition Cost (WAC).  Medication pricing by Elsevier, accessed January 2024."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -12,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 2,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "d.",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 12
        }
       }
      ],
      "tabPt": 12
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Limited available evidence suggests that abruptly switching (i.e., direct switch) from one "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "short-acting "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "SSRI or SNRI to another SSRI or SNRI is generally well-tolerated."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "3,7,10"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  Transient serotonergic side effects (e.g., anxiety) may occur early in the switch, but this is not usually a safety issue, and a direct switch is usually better tolerated than a washout if the first agent is short-acting.  "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "TAPERING/CROSS-TAPERING "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "(i.e., gradually increasing the new agent [often starting with a lower dose than usual] while decreasing the first agent):"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "22"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  Tapering may be more appropriate in some cases due to two concerns when switching:  symptom recurrence and discontinuation syndromes."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "12,30"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  Discontinuation syndromes are of most concern when switching from a serotonergic agent to a nonserotonergic agent, particularly when switching "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "from venlafaxine "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "or "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "paroxetine"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "2,7"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " Consider tapering any antidepressant taken for more than one week."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "27"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  Fluoxetine and bupropion may not need tapering."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "6,26,27"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  For others, consider tapering over several weeks unless there is a clinical reason not to."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "30"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  Monitor patient and adjust switching strategy (e.g., speed of taper) based on symptoms of withdrawal, side effects, or return of depressive symptoms."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "2,10"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  Consider increasing the dose of the serotonergic agent if withdrawal symptoms emerge (e.g., “GI flu”-like symptoms, paresthesias, irritability, insomnia, dizziness, vivid dreams)."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "10"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  Individual symptoms could also be treated (e.g., meclizine for dizziness)."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 12
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "27"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  A resource for switching is "
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 12
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "https://switchrx.com/antidepressants.php/switch"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -12,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 2,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "e.",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 12
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Gepirone ["
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "italic"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Exxua"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": ", US] is a selective serotonin 5HT1A receptor agonist."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "44"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  It has not been shown to be more effective than other antidepressants, and is more expensive than generic first-line agents.  Long-term data is limited.  Common adverse effects include dizziness, nausea, and headache."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "44"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " It requires baseline and periodic electrocardiographic monitoring due to risk of QT prolongation, and has significant interactions with CYP3A4 inhibitors."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "44"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "  Additionally, dose adjustments are required for older adults and for patients with kidney or liver impairment."
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "vertAlign",
        "attrs": {
         "value": "sup"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "44"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 0,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": 0,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 2,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": null
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       },
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Abbreviations"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 10
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": ":  CHF = congestive heart failure; GI = gastrointestinal; MAOI = monoamine oxidase inhibitor; SSRI = selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor; SNRI = serotonin norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor; TCA = tricyclic antidepressant."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
